Stream API

A Stream is NOT a data structure.
It doesn't store data.

Instead:
A Stream is a way of processing data.

Suppose:

List<Integer> numbers =
        Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30, 40, 50);

You want numbers greater than 25.

Without Stream:
    for(Integer number : numbers) {
        if(number > 25) {
            System.out.println(number);
        }
    }

With Stream:
    numbers.stream()
           .filter(number -> number > 25)
           .forEach(System.out::println);

Output:
        30
        40
        50

Imagine an office processing employees:

Employees
   ↓
Filter
   ↓
Transform
   ↓
Sort
   ↓
Collect

Java: employees.stream()
               .filter(...)
               .map(...)
               .sorted(...)
               .collect(...);

That's a Stream pipeline.


1) filter() means:

Keep only elements satisfying a condition.

Example:

List<Integer> numbers =
        Arrays.asList(10, 15, 20, 25, 30);

List<Integer> result =
        numbers.stream()
               .filter(n -> n > 20)
               .collect(Collectors.toList());

Result:

[25, 30]

map() means:

Transform each element into something else.

Example:

List<String> names =
        Arrays.asList("sneha", "rahul", "amit");

Convert to uppercase:

        List<String> result =
                names.stream()
                     .map(name -> name.toUpperCase())
                     .collect(Collectors.toList());

Result:

        [SNEHA, RAHUL, AMIT]

filter() is used to select elements based on a condition,
whereas map() is used to transform each element into another form.

Sorted() :
Sort elements.

numbers.stream()
       .sorted()
       .forEach(System.out::println);

Descending:

numbers.stream()
       .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
       .forEach(System.out::println);

distinct()

Remove duplicates.

List<Integer> numbers =
        Arrays.asList(10, 20, 10, 30, 20);

Then:

numbers.stream()
       .distinct()
       .forEach(System.out::println);

       Output:

       10
       20
       30

limit()

Take only a certain number.

numbers.stream()
       .limit(3)
       .forEach(System.out::println);


skip()

Skip elements.

numbers.stream()
       .skip(2)
       .forEach(System.out::println);

forEach()

Perform an action for every element.

numbers.stream()
       .forEach(System.out::println);

collect()

This is extremely important.

Streams process data, but eventually you often want the result as a:

List
Set
Map

Example:

List<Integer> result =
        numbers.stream()

filter(n -> n > 20)
               .collect(Collectors.toList());

So:

Stream processing
       ↓
collect()
       ↓
List

reduce()

This is another important interview topic.

Suppose:

List<Integer> numbers =
        Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30, 40);

You want:

10 + 20 + 30 + 40

Using: reduce()
       int sum =
           numbers.stream()
                  .reduce(0, (a, b) -> a + b);

       Result:

       100

       Think:

       Reduce many values into one value.

10 20 30 40 -> 100

Stream operations: memorize this classification
-------------------------------------------------------

1) Intermediate operations

They return another Stream.

filter
map
sorted
distinct
limit
skip

Example: stream() .filter(...) .map(...) .sorted(...)

2) Terminal operations

They finish the Stream.

forEach
collect
reduce
count
findFirst
anyMatch
allMatch
noneMatch

